## 4. Page structure (top to bottom)

This is what a Nail Feed post actually renders, in order:

1. **Share button** (floating, fixed position)
2. **Hero** — category label, title (H1), byline, date, then the hero image (16:9)
3. **Breadcrumbs** (`Home / Nail Feed / {title}`)
4. **Table of Contents** (mobile only, auto-built from `<h2>` headings in the body)
5. **Wrap Up card** (`tldr` — summary / key takeaways / short FAQ accordion) — only if `tldr` is set
6. **Body, first half** — content up to the nearest `<h2>` at/after the midpoint
7. **Inline newsletter bar** + **social follow icons (5 platforms)** — only if there's a second half to show (i.e. skipped entirely on posts with 0–1 `<h2>` headings)
8. **Body, second half** — remaining content
9. **FAQ section** (`faqItems`) — only if present
10. **Final newsletter** (larger, name+email form) — always shown
11. **Author card** — only if `author` resolves to a known author (see §6)
12. **About Mirellé Inspo** (static, same on every post)
13. **Read More** (related posts rail, see below)

### How body content splits into "first half" / "second half"

The compiled HTML is split at the **`<h2>` heading closest to the
midpoint** of all `<h2>` blocks — not an exact 50/50 character split, and
not `/blog`'s 33/34/33 pattern. A post needs **at least 2 `<h2>` headings**
for the mid-content newsletter/social block to appear at all; with 0 or 1
`<h2>`, all content renders as one block and that middle newsletter+social
section is skipped entirely (there's no "middle" to break at).

**Practical implication for prompt-writing: structure every post with at
least 2–3 `<h2>` sections**, both so the Table of Contents is useful and so
the mid-content engagement block actually shows up.

### Read More / related posts logic

Priority order, capped at 6 posts, no duplicates:
1. Posts listed explicitly in `topicalMap.relatedClusters`
2. Other posts with the same `topic`
3. Most recent posts (fallback filler)

