## 5. Hero image — hard requirement (Discover eligibility)

- **1200×675 minimum, 16:9 aspect ratio.** This is not a style preference —
  it's a Google Discover technical gate. Google requires ≥1200px width,
  16:9 ratio, >300,000 total pixels for large-image Discover eligibility.
  `/blog` uses 1200×630 — **do not reuse that ratio here.**
- Only the hero image needs this spec. Body images can be whatever fits
  the content.
- `imageWidth`/`imageHeight` in frontmatter should match the actual file
  dimensions — these values also feed the OG/Twitter card image metadata
  and the BlogPosting schema's image object.
- The site-wide `max-image-preview:large` robots directive is already
  correctly set in `src/app/layout.tsx` (under `robots.googleBot`) —
  nothing needed per-post for that part.

---

## 6. Author field — exact matching required

`author` in frontmatter is matched by **`resolveAuthor()`** in
`src/data/authors.ts`, using **case-insensitive substring matching**, not
an exact-match lookup:

- Contains `"jyoti"` or `"jyotika"` → resolves to Jyotika Verma
- Contains `"ri"` or `"gaurav"` → resolves to Gaurav Verma
- Anything else → resolves to nothing, and **the entire Author Card section
  silently does not render** (no error, no placeholder — it just isn't there)

**Use one of these two exact strings:** `"Jyotika Verma"` or
`"Gaurav Verma"`. Don't invent a third author or get creative with
formatting (initials, nicknames not listed above, etc.) — there is
currently no way to add a new author without also updating
`src/data/authors.ts`, which is out of scope for a content-only workflow.

Note the substring match is loose by design in the original code (not
something introduced for Nail Feed) — e.g. any string containing "ri"
matches Gaurav. Stick to the two exact full names above and this isn't a
practical issue.

