## 7. Word count & body image targets

Deliberately **different from `/blog`**, which targets 2200–3500 words
(pillar/spoke SEO depth). Nail Feed is scroll-driven, fast-consumption
content:

- **Target: 900–1300 words.**
- **Body images: 2–5**, light — scale up toward the higher end only for
  roundup-style posts, fewer for opinion/Q&A-style posts. Don't force a
  gallery structure; there is no gallery/carousel component wired into
  this template.

---

## 8. Headline guidance

- Negative-angle-plus-solution titles (e.g. *"your gel keeps lifting —
  here's why"*) and "trending now" style titles are fine, **but the post
  must actually deliver on the promise in the title.** Google's Discover
  ranking includes a classifier comparing headline promise against actual
  content — a title that overpromises and underdelivers gets suppressed,
  not rewarded, regardless of how good the headline itself reads.
- **~60–65 characters** is roughly where Discover truncates titles in the
  feed (not a hard platform limit, just where display starts clipping) —
  keep titles inside that if possible.

---

## 9. What NOT to carry over from `/blog`

If the agent generating prompts has also seen `/blog`'s content
conventions, explicitly avoid porting these over:

- `/blog`'s word count (2200–3500) — too long for Discover's format
- `/blog`'s topic-folder URL nesting — Nail Feed is flat
- `/blog`'s "inspo gallery" auto-linking after body images — doesn't exist
  in this route, don't reference `/inspo/...` URLs
- `/blog`'s multiple templates (`center`/`split-left`/`split-right`/`grid`)
  — Nail Feed has exactly one template, no `template` field
- `/blog`'s two-inline-newsletter-plus-final pattern at fixed 33/34/33
  splits — Nail Feed uses one mid-content break (position varies by `<h2>`
  count) plus the final newsletter
- Product grids, carousels, download buttons, affiliate banners, lightbox
  galleries — none of these are wired into the Nail Feed template

